---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import LifecycleDiagram from '../components/LifecycleDiagram';
import StressGauge from '../components/StressGauge';
import PhasePill from '../components/PhasePill.astro';
import SleepCycle from '../components/diagrams/SleepCycle.astro';
import HomeostaticLoop from '../components/diagrams/HomeostaticLoop.astro';
import StressMath from '../components/diagrams/StressMath.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Lifecycle — AnimaOS"
  description="The homeostatic loop, the four sleep phases, transition triggers, and the dreaming subsystem."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="03 — Lifecycle"
    title="The soul of Anima: a homeostatic loop."
    description="A living system maintains its internal state within bounds by continuous feedback rather than by external command. Anima applies this principle to an LLM agent: rather than running flat-out until OOM-killed and restarting, the system observes its own internal pressures and adjusts."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Cycle</span>
        <h2>Two macro-states. Four sleep sub-states.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Transitions are driven by the stress index, the task agenda, and
          explicit policy signals from <code>/dev/anima/senses/human</code>. The
          diagram below is interactive — click any phase to inspect its trigger
          and description.
        </p>
      </div>

      <LifecycleDiagram client:visible />

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <SleepCycle />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Waking</span>
        <h2>The somatic execution loop.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          When the system initialises, the agent boots as the primary lifecycle
          supervisor at PID 1. It reads its operational bounds from the human
          sensory peripheral and enters its main execution loop. All complexity
          is delegated to the subsystems — <code>vita</code> is the integrator.
        </p>
      </div>

      <HomeostaticLoop />

      <div class="card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <pre><code>{`// crates/vita/src/loop.rs
pub async fn somatic_execution_loop(
    lifecycle: &mut LifecycleManager,
    monitor: &HomeostaticMonitor,
) -> Result<(), LifecycleError> {
    loop {
        // 1. Read external human guidance changes from the sensory peripheral
        let human_guidance = lifecycle.senses.read_active_bounds().await?;
        lifecycle.update_policy_bounds(human_guidance);

        // 2. Query interoceptive metrics to evaluate physical stress
        let active_tokens = lifecycle.memory.get_l1_token_count();
        let stress_index = monitor.compute_systemic_stress_index(
            active_tokens, lifecycle.config.max_context,
        );

        // 3. Autonomous decision: should we transition to sleep?
        if lifecycle.agenda.is_empty() && stress_index < 0.4 {
            lifecycle.transition_to_sleep_state().await?;
            continue;
        }

        // 4. Emergency consolidation under critical stress
        if stress_index >= 0.9 {
            lifecycle.emergency_consolidate().await?;
            continue;
        }

        // 5. Select and dispatch the next task primitive
        if let Some(task) = lifecycle.agenda.select_optimal_task(stress_index) {
            lifecycle.scheduler.dispatch_task(task).await?;
        } else {
            // No high-priority tasks; yield to conserve resources
            tokio::task::yield_now().await;
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Stress-aware task selection</h3>
          <p>
            <code>select_optimal_task</code> is not a pure priority queue — it
            weighs candidate tasks against the current stress index and prefers
            tasks whose memory footprint and expected latency fit the current
            envelope.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>At <code>stress = 0.3</code>: dispatches long-form analysis happily.</li>
            <li>At <code>stress = 0.7</code>: prefers short turn-arounds; defers long tasks.</li>
            <li>A long task already running is <strong>not</strong> interrupted. The selector only chooses what to start next.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Human guidance as bounds, not commands</h3>
          <p>
            Bounds read from <code>/dev/anima/senses/human</code> are not
            commands. They are constraints the agent agrees to operate within.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>max_context_target: 0.5</code> — keep L1 ≤ half capacity.</li>
            <li><code>min_sleep_interval: 30m</code> — never go longer than this without a sleep cycle.</li>
            <li><code>priority_class: code_generation</code> — bias task selection toward this class.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Sleep · the four phases</span>
        <h2>Pruning. Replay. Dreaming. Compilation.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Sleep is not idle time. It is when the system does the work that would
          be catastrophic to interleave with active reasoning — eviction,
          validation, association discovery, training-data compilation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="phases">
        <PhasePill n="1" name="Pruning" color="#7df9c8" desc="Emotional decay is applied to L1 and L2. Below-threshold entries are evicted or compressed. The semantic floor protects high-generation knowledge from erasure." />
        <PhasePill n="2" name="Replay" color="#5dd1ff" desc="Sampled past questions are re-run against the pruned memory. If accuracy drops below threshold, the pruning is rolled back. Proof that the validation path works is an exit criterion for Phase 3." />
        <PhasePill n="3" name="Dreaming" color="#c084fc" desc="Random graph walks across L3 produce candidate associative edges. Yield is variable by design; candidates are validated in the next pruning cycle, not at production time." />
        <PhasePill n="4" name="Compilation" color="#ffb454" desc="Waking-state traces are compiled into training-data formats and persisted under training_corpus/ in L3. The sleep cycle closes; the agent transitions back to Waking." />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="eyebrow">Emergency consolidation</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1rem;">When stress ≥ 0.9.</h3>
          <p>
            Critical stress bypasses the full sleep cycle. Emergency consolidation
            performs an immediate L1 → L2 demotion of the lowest-activation
            entries, releases attention-field capacity, and returns the loop to
            normal operation without a full Waking → Sleeping transition.
          </p>
          <p class="muted small">
            This is a deliberate trade-off: the agent stays responsive to
            high-priority inputs at the cost of skipping replay and dreaming
            until the next scheduled sleep window.
          </p>
        </div>
        <StressGauge client:visible />
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <StressMath />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="card highlight-card">
        <span class="badge badge-purple mono">design note</span>
        <h3 style="margin: 0.75rem 0;">Why dreaming, and why it is variable by design.</h3>
        <p>
          Random walks across L3 produce mostly nothing. That is fine. The
          dreaming yield is the long tail of associative edges that the agent
          would never have explicitly searched for; most are useless, and the
          next pruning cycle filters them out. The cost of a failed candidate is
          a few bytes in L3. The cost of a successful candidate is an insight
          that ordinary retrieval would have missed.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          This is the same trade-off biological dream sleep appears to make. The
          mechanism is different; the policy is similar.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .section-head {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .section-head h2 { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .lede {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .phases {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .phases { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  @media (max-width: 500px) { .phases { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .highlight-card {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-3);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bg-1), rgba(192, 132, 252, 0.04));
  }
</style>
